C/ Isolates 239-STARTUP.hal's `SCHEDULE ... ON (ORBIT & (ORBIT2 & ORBIT3))`
C/ construct (a compound BAND event-expression) -- proves WORKER only
C/ starts once ALL THREE events are true, not as soon as the first one
C/ is (verified by SIGNALing them one at a time, with a WRITE after each,
C/ and WORKER itself WRITEing once it actually starts).
C/
C/ Scheduling is non-pre-emptive (USA003087 Sec. 13.1: a READY process is
C/ "barred" while another runs), so a WAIT after each SIGNAL is what yields
C/ to the scheduler and lets a now-armed WORKER actually dispatch -- exactly
C/ as the real 239-STARTUP context provides a yield.  If the compound event
C/ is not yet satisfied the WAIT dispatches nothing; when E3 completes the
C/ AND, WORKER runs during that WAIT, before the following WRITE.

  TSCHDAND: PROGRAM;
     DECLARE E1 EVENT LATCHED, E2 EVENT LATCHED, E3 EVENT LATCHED;

  WORKER:
  TASK;
     WRITE(6) 'WORKER STARTED';
  CLOSE WORKER;

     SCHEDULE WORKER ON (E1 & (E2 & E3)) PRIORITY(70);
     WRITE(6) 'BEFORE ANY SIGNAL';
     SIGNAL E1;
     WAIT(5);
     WRITE(6) 'AFTER E1';
     SIGNAL E2;
     WAIT(5);
     WRITE(6) 'AFTER E2';
     SIGNAL E3;
     WAIT(5);
     WRITE(6) 'AFTER E3';
  CLOSE TSCHDAND;
